%! Blank nodes and nulls.
%!
%! A blank node such as _:home is a value that exists but has no name.
%! Every mention of _:home in a file is the same value, and _:other
%! would be another. A blank node belongs to the file it is written in:
%! every use of a module has blank nodes of its own, as every RDF file
%! Nemo reads has.
%!
%! An existential variable such as !y invents values, too, but a new one
%! for every match of its rule, and only where no fact provides one.
%!
%! Here, the modules are the input files household.rls and vehicles.rls
%! (see "Input files" next to the run button), which `@use household`
%! and `@use vehicles` read.
%!
%! The example shows:
%! - how a blank node is one value, however often a rule uses it,
%! - how every use of a module gets blank nodes of its own,
%! - how blank nodes describe the classes without a name in an ontology,
%! - how an existential variable !y invents a value only where needed.

@use household { member = millers } as millerHouse .
@use household { member = smiths } as smithHouse .

millers(anna) .
millers(ben) .
smiths(carl) .

livesAt(?person, ?place) :- millerHouse::livesAt(?person, ?place) .
livesAt(?person, ?place) :- smithHouse::livesAt(?person, ?place) .

% The module writes _:home for both households, but each use has its
% own: Anna and Ben share a home, and Carl lives elsewhere. Had the
% module written a name such as home, Carl would live with them, too.
housemates(?x, ?y) :- livesAt(?x, ?place), livesAt(?y, ?place), ?x != ?y .

% A use case: an ontology in the shape OWL takes in RDF. "Every bicycle
% has some wheel as a part" makes bicycle a subclass of a class without
% a name, the things with some wheel as a part. RDF writes such a class
% as a blank node, and so does this program:
axiom(bicycle, subClassOf, _:r) .
axiom(_:r, onProperty, hasPart) .
axiom(_:r, someValuesFrom, wheel) .

% vehicles.rls adds that every motorbike has some motor as a part, and
% calls its class _:r, too, as tools that write RDF often do. The two
% stay apart; had both files written the name r, every bicycle would
% need a motor and every motorbike a wheel.
@use vehicles as more .
axiom(?s, ?p, ?o) :- more::axiom(?s, ?p, ?o) .

type(myBike, bicycle) .
type(yourBike, bicycle) .
type(myMoped, motorbike) .
related(yourBike, hasPart, frontWheel) .
type(frontWheel, wheel) .

% What a class asks for, its members have. Nemo invents a wheel for
% myBike and a motor for myMoped, but no wheel for yourBike, whose
% wheel is known:
type(?x, ?d) :- type(?x, ?c), axiom(?c, subClassOf, ?d) .
related(?x, ?p, !y), type(!y, ?c) :-
    type(?x, ?r), axiom(?r, onProperty, ?p), axiom(?r, someValuesFrom, ?c) .
parts(?thing, ?part, ?class) :- related(?thing, hasPart, ?part), type(?part, ?class) .

@output livesAt, housemates, parts .
